      "content": "In the realm of database management, data integrity is not merely a goal but a fundamental requirement, especially when multiple users attempt to access and modify the same data simultaneously. To understand how systems maintain this integrity, we must explore three interconnected concepts: transaction isolation, race conditions, and deadlock prevention.\n\n### Transaction Isolation\n\nTransaction isolation is the property that ensures concurrent transactions do not interfere with each other. It defines the degree to which the operations of one transaction are visible to others. The SQL standard defines four isolation levels, ranging from the least to the most restrictive:\n\n1. **Read Uncommitted**: Transactions can see uncommitted changes from other transactions. This is the fastest but least safe level, prone to \"dirty reads.\"\n2. **Read Committed**: Transactions can only see committed data. This prevents dirty reads but allows \"non-repeatable reads\" (where a row changes between two reads in the same transaction).\n3. **Repeatable Read**: Ensures that if a transaction reads a row, it will see the same value if it reads it again, even if other transactions have modified it. This prevents non-repeatable reads but may allow \"phantom reads\" (where new rows appear in a range query).\n4. **Serializable**: The strict",
